    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let's dive into the physics of it. When you produce an /s/ sound, the air flowing through the glottis (the space between the vocal cords) creates a high-speed jet of air that passes through a narrow opening, causing turbulence and friction. This turbulence is what produces the characteristic hissing sound of /s/.\n\nIn contrast, when you produce a /z/ sound, the air flowing through the glottis creates a lower-speed jet of air that passes through a more open space, resulting in less turbulence and more vibration. This increased vibration causes the air to vibrate more, producing the characteristic buzzing sound of /z/.\n\nTo be more specific, when you produce an /s/ sound, the air flowing through the glottis creates a \"supraglottal\" airflow, where the air is accelerated and then decelerated rapidly, creating the turbulence and friction that produces the /s/ sound. When you produce a /z/ sound, the air flowing through the glottis creates an \"intra-glottal\" airflow, where the air is accelerated and then decelerated more slowly, resulting in more vibration and the /z/ sound.\n\nThink of it like a narrow pipe and a wider",
